4. Elements of Programming Interviews in Java

Authors: Adnan Aziz, Tsung-Hsien Lee, and Amit Prakash
 Level: Intermediate to Advanced
 Time: 8 to 10 weeks
 Priority: Best Java interview book

This is the strongest single book for a Java developer who wants rigorous interview preparation. It combines Java, data structures, algorithms, problem-solving strategies, and interview-oriented exercises.

Recommended reading order

Do not follow the chapters strictly from start to finish. Use this order:

Primitive types
Arrays
Strings
Linked lists
Stacks and queues
Hash tables
Binary trees
Binary search trees
Heaps
Searching
Sorting
Recursion
Greedy algorithms
Graphs
Dynamic programming
Parallel computing
Three-pass technique
Pass 1: Understand
Read the problem
Attempt it for 20 to 30 minutes
Read the explanation if blocked
Reimplement without copying
Pass 2: Recall

Return after three to seven days and solve it without notes.

Pass 3: Interview simulation

Explain and code the solution in 30 to 40 minutes without IDE assistance.

Practical milestone

Complete:

All recommended starter problems
Most medium-difficulty problems
Carefully selected advanced problems
At least two problems from every major topic

Target: Approximately 100 to 120 well-understood problems.

Phase 4: Strengthen General Algorithm Design
5. The Algorithm Design Manual, 3rd Edition

Author: Steven S. Skiena
 Level: Advanced
 Time: 6 to 8 weeks
 Priority: Essential for deeper problem-solving

This book teaches how to choose and design algorithms, not merely implement standard data structures. It is consistently recommended alongside CLRS, Sedgewick, Weiss, and interview-oriented books.

Focus areas
Correct algorithm modeling
Big-O analysis
Data-structure selection
Sorting and searching
Graph traversal
Weighted graph algorithms
Combinatorial search
Dynamic programming
Greedy algorithms
Intractable problems
Approximation and heuristics
Why read it after interview patterns?

Pattern books help you recognize familiar structures. Skiena helps when a problem does not fit a memorized pattern.

